{
  "rules": [
    "Strong persistent underprediction in bullish regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend, the model level is systematically too low versus realized prices (often by ~5–12 EUR across the 10–30d horizon). Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast follows the direction but not the slope; realized paths accelerate upward while the model stays comparatively flat. Add a momentum/acceleration term (e.g., proportional to recent 5–10d return and its change) to steepen the forward curve.",
    "Horizon-specific bias increases with time: near-term errors are smaller, but underprediction widens materially beyond ~day 7–10 and is largest by day 20–30. Use a horizon-dependent uplift (increasing function of lead time) rather than a constant bias correction.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after breakout: after an upside break, the model repeatedly pulls back toward ~69–72 while the market continues trending to ~80–84. Suppress mean-reversion strength when price is making higher highs/higher lows (trend filter).",
    "Volatility/peak underestimation: the model misses the magnitude of peaks during rallies (tops are too low and too early). Inflate projected volatility/upside tail when recent realized volatility and momentum are both elevated."
  ]
}
